# The Round - a night cleaner, fourteen rooms, and one that is always already clean.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, and the list that has to agree with the building.

title: The Round
start: start

== start ==
bg east_hall
music room_tone
Four hours, fourteen rooms, the same order for six years: the two floors, the washrooms,
the stairs, the kitchens, and the cupboard at the end where the trolley lives.
The list is on card in the cupboard, in your own handwriting, because the
building changed hands twice and nobody ever printed a new one.
Fourteen lines. You do them in order. That is the whole job.
-> the_first_floor

== the_first_floor ==
The first floor is offices: nine of them, all the same shape, all with a bin and
a desk and a window that looks at the car park.
Seven are used. Two are not, and have not been for as long as you have worked
here, and you do them anyway, because they are on the list.
Tonight the eighth office is empty in a way the others are not: cleared. Desk,
chair, the little shelf, the picture hook, all of it gone, and the carpet has
four pale rectangles where the desk legs stood.
* Do it anyway. It is on the list. -> do_it
* Look in the other unused office. -> the_ninth
* Ask the security desk in the morning. -> morning

== the_ninth ==
The ninth office was cleared years ago and you have cleaned it every night of
those years. There is nothing in it. There is a radiator, and a window, and a
smell of dust that you have decided is the building rather than the room.
You stand in it for a minute with the trolley in the doorway, which is a
minute nobody is paying you for.
* Do the eighth anyway. -> do_it
* Leave the eighth and come back to it at the end. -> come_back

== do_it ==
You do it. Bin, desk gone, so just the carpet, and you vacuum the four pale
rectangles because the machine does not know the difference between a clear
floor and a furnished one.
The eighth office is on the list. It is not your job to decide whether a room
should be there. It is your job to leave it the way the list says a room is.
-> the_washrooms

== come_back ==
You do the round in order and you leave the eighth for last, which is against
the order and which you have not done in six years.
You come back at half past four with the trolley and the light off, and the
eighth office is clean. Vacuumed, in four pale rectangles, and the bin emptied,
and the window, and everything the way you do it, and you have not been in it.
-> ending_someone

== morning ==
In the morning you stand at the security desk with your card and you ask about
the eighth office, and the man on the desk looks at the book, because that is
what the desk has: a book.
security: Eight was vacated on the fourteenth. Contractors took the furniture
out in the afternoon. Nobody's been up since.
you: I cleaned it last night.
security: You clean everything.
you: I know.
* Do it anyway, tonight, and keep doing it. -> do_it
* Take the line off the card. -> take_it_off
* Leave the line on the card and stop going in. -> ending_quiet

== the_washrooms ==
The washrooms are the washrooms: eight sinks, four cubicles a side, and the
floor that needs doing properly because it is the only floor anybody notices.
Then the stairs, then the kitchens, and the kitchens are the good part because
of the windows and the quiet, and then the cupboard, where the trolley goes and
where the card is.
You tick nothing, because you have never ticked anything. You look at the card
the way you look at it every night, and tonight you count the lines.
Fourteen, and always fourteen.
-> ending_counted

== take_it_off ==
You take the pen off the trolley and you draw a line through the eighth office,
and it takes a second, and then the card says thirteen and looks wrong, and the
building does not feel any different at all.
You do the thirteenth room and you put the pen back in the cup and you go home
in the dark, and that is that, and it is the right thing, and you know it is.
It is on the list tomorrow because you have not got round to writing out a
clean card. It is on the list next week too. The pen stays in the cup.
-> END

== ending_counted ==
Fourteen, and always fourteen, and there are nine offices, and two of them have
not been used since before the building changed hands twice, and one of them was
cleared on the fourteenth by contractors who have been up once.
You do the washrooms. You do the stairs. You do the kitchens, and you stand at
the kitchen window for longer than the job needs, because it is the good part
and nobody is counting.
-> END

== ending_quiet ==
You keep the line on the card and you stop opening the door. The trolley goes
past it four times a night and the carpet inside gets whatever the corridor
gets, which is nothing, because the corridor gets done by you.
In November somebody puts a sign on the eighth door, and in January the corridor
outside it is part of the round by everybody's account, and you have never once
said anything about any of it, which is the whole of the arrangement you have
with this building.
-> END

== ending_someone ==
You leave it clean and you do not write anything down, and for six weeks the
eighth office is on the list and clean by the time you get to it, and after six
weeks it is not, and you go back to doing it, and you are old enough to know
that both of those mean the same thing.
The list stays at fourteen. You have never been asked about it. You put the
trolley away at five past five and you say goodnight to the building, which is
something you started doing years ago and could not tell you when.
-> END
